! jb_ax5b "I-Box 5", routine 1260's TS block -- the deck's own test
! that the INTERVAL TIMER counts down and raises Time Signal.  PDF
! p.41 (printed p.38), octal 016007.00-016012.40, transcribed VERBATIM
! at the listing's own addresses and checked bit-exactly by
! verify_ax5b_16071_listing.py.
!
! WHAT TS IS, from the primary source.  Reference Manual p.44,
! "Attention Request": "4. Time Signal (TS).  The interval timer has
! become zero."  Eight words, and they explain every instruction here.
!
! HOW THE BLOCK WORKS, and this one IS traceable end to end -- every
! step below is printed somewhere, none of it read off a symbol's name:
!   LX,$X4,BIT18       load the whole word at BIT18.  The BIT table is
!                      printed on PDF p.114 under "-COMMON CONSTANTS
!                      FOR FETCH ONLY": BIT<n> is the word whose only
!                      set bit is bit n, one word per bit from
!                      BIT0 at 024522.00, so BIT18 is at 024544.00 --
!                      which is exactly what this row's operand
!                      prints.  BIT18's own row reads
!                      "XW,(8).40,0,0" assembling to
!                      "0.40 00 000000.00 00": bits 0-17 zero, octal
!                      pair 40 = 100000, i.e. BIT 18 SET.  The value
!                      is read, not inferred from the name.
!   SV,$X4,1.0         store that value into word 1.  The pocket card
!                      X22-6533-1's special-storage table (transcribed
!                      first-hand in docs/microarchitecture_plan.md
!                      A.10) puts the INTERVAL TIMER at word 1 bits
!                      0-18, so bit 18 is its low-order bit and the
!                      timer is set to ONE -- one tick from zero.
!                      The card's footnote (a) on that row says the
!                      timer is "read-only except for store-value/
!                      count/refill/address", so SV is not an
!                      arbitrary choice of store: it is one of the few
!                      operations that can write this register at all.
!
!   AND THE DECK MUST BE RUNNING DISABLED HERE, which these eight rows
!   do NOT show.  Word 1 is one of Reference Manual p.31's permanently
!   protected locations, and p.45 raises DS on a to-memory operation
!   into that area "while the interruption system was enabled", which
!   also suppresses the store.  So this SV lands only with the system
!   off.  That is entailed by two manual pages, not transcribed from
!   this page -- the BD that does it is outside the range.
!
!   A SYMMETRY WORTH NOTING.  16086 (ax5b_16086_ds.strap) uses
!   "SV,$X4,1.00" as its DS PROVOCATION, enabled, expecting the store
!   to be suppressed.  This block uses the same instruction on the
!   same word as a legitimate timer load, disabled, expecting it to
!   land.  Same instruction, opposite intent, distinguished only by
!   the enable bit -- which is exactly the two-directional rule
!   storage_protection.strap tests.
!   LCI,$X4,512        a count of 512...
!   CB,$X4,$           ...counted down by a COUNT AND BRANCH that
!                      branches to ITSELF.  That is the wait: 512
!                      iterations for the timer to reach zero.
!   BTS,16088          Time Signal on?  Then on to the indicator walk
!                      at 16088.  Otherwise SIC,SEN / B,SERS reports
!                      "UNABLE TO TURN TS ON." and B,16093 moves to
!                      the next test.
!
! WHY IT IS WORTH TRANSCRIBING.  docs/NEXT.md §5 defers "the interval
! timer actually ticking" and names routine 1258 as its deck oracle.
! This is a SECOND oracle for the same hardware, eight rows long,
! inside a routine already being transcribed -- and it is far smaller
! than 1258.
!
! THREE ENCODINGS IT PINS.
!   * BTS "21400.42 42" -- branch on indicator 4.  With BOP (15) and
!     BAD (16) in ax5b_16082_op_ad.strap and BDS (19) / BADZ (16) in
!     ax5b_16086_ds.strap, that is four widely-spaced indicator
!     numbers through a field that straddles the listing's printed
!     octal/hex boundary.  Moving TS from 4 to 5 changes ONLY the hex
!     byte, the same way moving OP from 15 to 14 does -- the two ends
!     of the range fail in the same field, which is what a straddling
!     field looks like from either side.
!
!     This is also a FIFTH witness that 16088 is at 021400.40: ".42"
!     is 100010, bit 18 set.  As in the other two blocks, L16088's
!     .40 is a SYN below -- an INPUT to the check -- so the passing
!     row is not the witness; the printed code read at 600 dpi is.
!   * LCI "1000.11 02" -- 512 decimal is octal 1000, printed in the
!     WORD-address column because LCI's operand is an 18-bit count in
!     the top 18 bits of the 19-bit field, not an address (Reference
!     Manual p.25).  strap/README.md's Gotchas item 16 records that
!     this exact field was once encoded wrongly and was caught by
!     verify_ibox1_listing.py; this is a second witness for it.
!   * CB "16010.50 48" -- a count-and-branch to ITSELF at 016010.40.
!     Bit 18 of the target is one, and "$" is the only operand on
!     these pages that can be checked without an external symbol.
!
! A CROSS-PAGE CHECK THAT THEN BECAME A PRINTED FACT.  BIT18 is
! 024544.00 here; on p.45 the indicator-walk driver's
! "TI,1,BIT0%$X1,$IND" prints its first operand as 24522, and
! 024544 - 024522 = 18 exactly, which made BIT<n> = 024522 + n a
! good guess.  p.114 then prints the table itself and the guess is
! right -- including BIT0 = 024522.00, which the eventual TI
! transcription of p.45 will need.  The same thing happened with
! 160K7/160K16 and 023041 + n, which is still only the arithmetic;
! see docs/archival_inventory.md for both.
!
! WHAT THIS IS AND IS NOT.  An ENCODING oracle, like the other two
! blocks from this routine.  It is NOT run on the model: the block
! falls through into the SEN/SERS error harness and 16093, none of it
! transcribed, and this model's interval timer does not tick (NEXT.md
! §5).  Running it is exactly what implementing the timer would make
! possible, which is the reason to have the oracle first.
!
! SUBSTITUTIONS: one, the same systematic one as the other two blocks.
! This deck labels its blocks with NUMBERS (16071, 16093, 16088) and
! this assembler reads a leading digit as an address, so every such
! label is prefixed with "L".  Nothing else is changed.

! External symbols, given by SYN at the addresses this same listing
! prints for them.  Every row but the CB names one, so the CB's "$"
! is this block's only internal address check.
BIT18   SYN, (8)24544.0            ! the constant whose bit 18 is one
SEN     SYN, (8)1310.0             ! the SIC error-entry link
SERS    SYN, (8)1304.0             ! the error-report routine
L16093  SYN, (8)21734.0            ! the "next test" exit
L16088  SYN, (8)21400.40           ! the per-indicator interrupt walk

        SLC, (8)16007.0
L16071  LX,  $X4, BIT18            ! -TS          the value one
        SV,  $X4, 1.0              !              into the interval timer
        LCI, $X4, 512              !              a count of 512...
        CB,  $X4, $                !              ...spun down on itself
        BTS, L16088                !              Time Signal on?
        SIC, SEN                   ! -UNABLE TO TURN TS ON.
        B,   SERS
        B,   L16093
        END, L16071
